7eb9db2b.description:
Authors prefetch.ts to override IntersectionObserver globally with rootMargin "300% 0px 300% 0px", causing FB's own pager to fire 2-3 screens ahead — the direct fix for the user-reported "FB stops me scrolling at ~9 entries" symptom. Opt-in toggle (broadband cost ~1.5x on continuous scroll). Re-measures with JankStats vs Phase 1 baseline.

Authors app/src/main/ts/prefetch.ts (compiled → res/raw/prefetch.js): document_start IntersectionObserver override:
(() => {
  const Orig = window.IntersectionObserver;
  if (!Orig) return;
  window.IntersectionObserver = class extends Orig {
    constructor(cb, options) {
      const o = options ?? {};
      if (!o.rootMargin || o.rootMargin === "0px" || o.rootMargin === "") {
        o.rootMargin = "300% 0px 300% 0px";
      }
      super(cb, o);
    }
  };
})();
Injects via WebViewCompat.addDocumentStartJavaScriptCompat(view, …, "*") — runs before FB's bundle constructs its own IOs.

Adds opt-in setting "Aggressive feed prefetch" in SettingsViewModel.kt; default off (broadband cost ~1.5x on continuous scroll).

Re-measures with JankStats (saved output as baselines/baseline_v2.txt); compares to baseline_v1.txt from Phase 1.2; documents deltas. Validates the reported "scroll-stop at ~9 entries" symptom is gone (target: seamless pagination to ~30+ entries before any hesitation).

Runs full regression: verifies each of the 13 user-facing toggles functions identically. Uses AdblockTest.kt Playwright harness with refreshed selectors against live FB (assumes refresh_fb_metadata.sh has been run once).

Sources: compressed block (b6) for the IO rootMargin-override fix (m0041 — root cause: FB pager fires only when sentinel enters viewport, so user must wait for network round-trip at every page boundary; bigger rootMargin causes prefetch 2-3 screens ahead). androidx.webkit addDocumentStartJavaScriptCompat: https://developer.android.com/reference/androidx/webkit/WebViewCompat.

Trade-off: bigger rootMargin prefetches more aggressively → ~1.5x bandwidth burst on continuous scroll. Opt-in mitigates. Combined with (b2) observer cleanup gets near-seamless scroll because main-thread jank was delaying QPL timing marks that gate pagination.

Acceptance criteria: zero scroll-jank observing FB's React-rendered feed on the user's specific hardware through ~30 entries of continuous fast scrolling, with aggressive-feeds toggle on.

